  "verse": 10,
  "plain_english": "**\"Thence the agent too is constructed\"**\n\nThe Buddhist's attack has been relentless. He has denied the self, denied the cognition, denied the\naction. Now he completes it. For what holds all these together — the *relation* between the knower and\nthe known, between the agent and the act — is itself impossible; and once the relation is gone, \"thence\nthe agent too is constructed.\" The agent, like everything else, is only a fabrication, a convenient\nconstruction with nothing real behind it.\n\nThe kārikā's \"thence\" gathers a fourfold absence as its reason: the absence of the action, the absence\nof the object, the absence of the relation, and the absence of any determination of being in any of\nthem. And the relation's absence is argued on its own terms. A relation would have to stand *in both*\nits terms — in the knower and the known at once. But the two-resting thing cannot be one: what stands\nin two cannot be a single thing, any more than the two jars in the two houses are one jar. Moreover,\nthe established does not expect another — a thing already complete does not reach out for a relation —\nand the dependence that a relation would impose is itself unfitting. So the relation is absent; and\nwith it, the agent that the relation would bind together is only constructed.\n\nAbhinavagupta pauses to make one methodological point, and it is important. The Buddhist thinks he\nrefutes the relation by a bare non-perception — \"I do not see it, so it is not.\" But a bare\nnon-perception proves non-being only within a restriction of place and time: \"here, now, there is no\njar.\" It cannot reach the universal non-being of the relation. To do that, one must show a real\ncontradiction in the opponent's own account — that the very form he ascribes to the relation is\ncontradicted by another form he ascribes to it. That is a refutation grounded in the thing itself\n(prameya-niṣṭha), not a mere failure to perceive.\n\nAnd this is where the pūrvapakṣa — the no-self attack — comes to its close. The Buddhist has shown,\nto his own satisfaction, that the self, the cognition, the action, and the relation all fall; and\n\"thence the agent too is constructed.\" The whole edifice of a real knower and doer is, for him, a\nfabrication.\n",
  "sanskrit_cite": "pāratantryādyayogācca tena kartāpi kalpitaḥ || 11 || | nanu dṛśyānupalapalabdhyaiva | yugapat tiṣṭhati, tadekaṃ na bhavati | veśmadvayavarti ghaṭadvayamiva",
